var tafsirData_71 = {
  "1": "“Indeed, We sent Noah to his people ˹saying to him˺, ‘Warn your people before a painful punishment comes to them.’” opens the surah that bears Noah's name, and the verse records the sending of Noah with the warning that began his mission. “Indeed, We sent Noah to his people saying to him, 'Warn your people before a painful punishment comes to them,'” or “inna arsalna Noohan ila qawmihi an andhir qawmaka min qabli an ya'tiyahum 'athabun aleem.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah was sent to his people with the command to warn them before the punishment came, and the believer is taught that the warning precedes the punishment, and that the messenger is sent not to bring the punishment but to prevent it, and that the warning is the mercy that stands between the people and the painful end. Al-Tabari notes that the warning was the first and constant content of Noah's mission, and the believer is taught that the prophet is the warner before he is anything else, and that the warning is the call to turn before the door of the turning closes.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “andhir” — “warn” — and explains that the warning of the prophet is the warning of the one who loves, and that Noah warned his people for centuries because he feared for them the punishment that awaited them, and the believer is taught that the warning of the messenger is not the threat of the enemy but the care of the brother, and that the one who warns has already done the work of love. The verse also teaches the believer the order of the divine dealing, for the punishment is sent only after the warning, and the believer is taught that no people is punished without the warning that precedes it, and that the warning is the proof that the punishment is just, and that the one who is warned and persists has no excuse before the punishment. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear the warning of the messengers as the warning addressed to himself, and to turn before the punishment, and to know that the warning is the mercy that the heedless refuse and the believer receives.",
  "2": "“Noah proclaimed, ‘O my people! I am truly sent to you with a clear warning:” records the opening of Noah's call, and the verse gives the first words with which the prophet addressed his people. “Noah proclaimed, 'O my people! I am truly sent to you with a clear warning,'” or “qala ya qawmi innee lakum natheerun mubeen.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah addressed his people with the words of kinship — “O my people” — and the believer is taught that the messenger is the brother of his people, and that the call begins with the bond of the kinship and not with the distance of the stranger, and that the warning which is delivered with love is the warning that reaches the heart. Al-Tabari notes the word “mubeen” — “clear” — and the believer is taught that the warning of Noah was not obscure but plain, and that the messenger leaves no room for the excuse of ignorance, and that the clarity of the warning is the measure of the mercy of the warner.\n\nAl-Razi observes the structure of the address, for Noah called his people “my people” and declared his mission with the two words “warner” and “clear,” and the believer is taught that the messenger is the one who is near to his people and plain with his people, and that the nearness and the plainness are the two wings of the call, and that the one who calls with love and clarity has done what the messenger does. The verse also teaches the believer the manner of the call, for the one who calls others to the truth must call them with the gentleness of the kinship and the clarity of the proof, and the believer is taught to make his own call a call of the brother and the warner, and to know that the warning which is loved is the warning which is heard. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to receive the call of the messenger as the call of the brother, and to answer it with the obedience of the one who is loved and warned, and to know that the clear warning is the mercy that the Lord sends before the punishment.",
  "3": "“worship Allah ˹alone˺, fear Him, and obey me.” states the content of the warning, and the verse gives the three commands that gather the whole of the religion. “Worship Allah alone, fear Him, and obey me,” or “ani 'budoo Allaha wattaqoohu wa atee'oon.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah commanded his people to worship God alone, to fear Him, and to obey him, and the believer is taught that these three are the pillars of the message, for the worship of God alone is the foundation, the fear of God is the guard, and the obedience to the messenger is the way, and the one who holds the three has held the whole of the religion. Al-Tabari notes the order of the three, for the worship comes first, then the fear, then the obedience, and the believer is taught that the worship of God is the root, and that the fear and the obedience are its branches, and that the one who worships God alone is the one who fears Him and obeys His messenger.\n\nAl-Razi observes the completeness of the three, for the worship addresses the heart, the fear addresses the inward state, and the obedience addresses the outward deed, and the believer is taught that the religion of God is the religion of the whole person, the heart and the state and the deed, and that the one who worships without fearing and the one who fears without obeying has taken a part of the religion and left the parts that complete it. The verse also teaches the believer the relation between the fear and the obedience, for the fear of God is the motive of the obedience, and the obedience to the messenger is the expression of the fear, and the believer is taught to join them, and to know that the fear which does not produce the obedience is the fear that is not yet fear. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make the three commands his own, to worship God alone, to fear Him, and to obey His messenger, and to know that the three are the one path, and that the one who walks it is the one who is saved from the warning that Noah delivered.",
  "4": "“He will forgive your sins, and delay your end until the appointed time. Indeed, when the time set by Allah comes, it cannot be delayed, if only you knew!’” states the reward of the obedience and the condition of the time, and the verse opens the door of the mercy before it speaks of the end. “He will forgive your sins, and delay your end until the appointed time. Indeed, when the time set by Allah comes, it cannot be delayed, if only you knew!” or “yaghfir lakum min thunubikum wa yu'akhkhirkum ila ajalin musamma inna ajala Allahi itha ja'a la yu'akhkhar law kuntum ta'lamoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah promised his people the forgiveness of their sins and the extension of their lives to the appointed term if they believed, and the believer is taught that the obedience of the believer brings the forgiveness of the past and the blessing of the present, and that the life of the believer is extended in its blessing even when its span is fixed. Al-Tabari notes the word “ajal” — “appointed term” — and the believer is taught that the term of every life is set by God, and that the setting is the wisdom, and that the delay and the hastening are in the hand of the Lord.\n\nAl-Razi observes the balance of the verse, for the promise of the forgiveness and the extension is followed by the warning of the fixed term, and the believer is taught that the mercy of God is offered within the limit of the term, and that the one who delays his turning is delaying within a term that will not delay, and that the wisdom of the believer is to turn before the term, and to know that the term which has come is the term which cannot be postponed. The verse also teaches the believer the value of the time, for the time is the field of the turning, and the believer is taught to fill his time with the obedience that brings the forgiveness, and to know that the time which is spent in the heedlessness is the time which is lost beyond the recovery. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to seize the time of the mercy, for the forgiveness is offered and the term is fixed, and the believer is taught to turn while the door is open, and to know that the door which is open today is the door that will close with the term.",
  "5": "“He cried, ‘My Lord! I have surely called my people day and night,” records the complaint of Noah to his Lord, and the verse opens the second movement of the surah, in which the prophet recounts the years of his call. “He cried, 'My Lord! I have surely called my people day and night,'” or “qala rabbi innee da'awtu qawmee laylan wa nahara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah turned to his Lord and recounted that he had called his people by night and by day, and the believer is taught that the messenger does not call at his convenience but at every hour, and that the call of Noah was the call of the unwearying, the call that filled the night and the day, and that the believer is taught to persist in the call to the good as Noah persisted, and to know that the persistence is the proof of the sincerity. Al-Tabari notes the order of the words — “night and day” — and the believer is taught that the call which fills the whole of the time is the call of the one who has given his time to the message.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the complaint, for the prophet who has called for so long turns to his Lord with the account of his effort, and the believer is taught that the complaint of the messenger to his Lord is not the complaint of the despairing but the complaint of the exhausted, and that the one who has done his duty may lay his effort before his Lord, and that the laying of the effort is the trust that the Lord will complete what the servant began. The verse also teaches the believer the patience of Noah, for the call of the night and the day was the call of centuries, and the believer is taught to measure his own patience by the patience of Noah, and to know that the work of the truth is the work of the long span, and that the one who wearies before the harvest has not learned the patience of the planter. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to persist in the good, to call to the truth in the night and the day, and to lay his effort before his Lord, and to know that the Lord who sees the effort completes it in His time.",
  "6": "“but my calls only made them run farther away.” records the result of Noah's ceaseless call, and the verse states the paradox that the surah preserves. “But my calls only made them run farther away,” or “fa-lam yazidhum du'a'ee illa firara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the more Noah called his people, the more they fled from him, and the believer is taught that the truth does not always win the hearer, and that the call which is made with love may be met with flight, and that the flight of the hearer is not the failure of the caller, and that the messenger is not judged by the response of his people but by the delivery of his message. Al-Tabari notes the word “firar” — “fleeing” — and the believer is taught that the heedless flee the truth as the sick flee the medicine, and that the flight is the symptom of the disease, and that the caller is to continue the call and not to despair of the healing.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the verse for the caller to the truth, for the believer is taught that the response to the call is not in his hand, and that the call which is refused is still the call that has done its work, and that the one who calls and is met with flight has shared the lot of the prophets, and that the flight of the people is the sign that the truth has reached them, and that the reaching is the success of the caller. The verse also teaches the believer the danger of the truth to the heedless, for the heedless flee the truth because the truth disturbs their ease, and the believer is taught to understand the flight of the heedless and not to take it as the proof of the falsity of the truth, and to know that the light which hurts the eye is still the light. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to continue the call in the face of the flight, and to know that the fleeing are fleeing the light, and that the caller is to hold the light and to trust that the Lord of the light opens the eyes in His time.",
  "7": "“And whenever I invite them to be forgiven by You, they press their fingers into their ears, cover themselves with their clothes, persist ˹in denial˺, and act very arrogantly.” describes the manner of their flight, and the verse gives the picture of the heedless closing every avenue of the call. “And whenever I invite them to be forgiven by You, they press their fingers into their ears, cover themselves with their clothes, persist in denial, and act very arrogantly,” or “wa innee kullama da'awtuhum li-taghfira lahum ja'aloo asabi'ahum fee athanihim wastaghshaw thiyabahum wa asarroo wastakbaroo istikbara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the people of Noah, when he called them to the forgiveness of God, put their fingers in their ears and wrapped themselves in their clothes, and the believer is taught that the heedless close the ear and the eye against the truth, and that the closing is the work of the one who has decided not to hear, and that the one who closes his ear has closed the door of the mercy that the call was opening. Al-Tabari notes the four acts — the fingers in the ears, the wrapping in the clothes, the persistence, and the arrogance — and the believer is taught that the four are the four walls that the heedless build against the truth, and that the walls are built by the heedless against their own salvation.\n\nAl-Razi observes the psychology of the closing, for the heedless close the ear because the truth disturbs them, and they wrap themselves because the light disturbs them, and they persist and grow arrogant because the persistence and the arrogance are the refuge of the one who will not change, and the believer is taught that the closing of the ear is the beginning of the hardening, and that the one who will not hear the truth will soon be unable to hear it. The verse also teaches the believer the patience of Noah, for the prophet saw his people closing their ears and wrapping themselves and he did not stop calling, and the believer is taught that the messenger calls even to the closed ear, and that the call is the duty whatever the response. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to keep his own ear open, and to fear the closing that the verse describes, and to know that the ear which is closed against the truth is the ear which is closed against the mercy, and the believer is taught to hear and to turn.",
  "8": "“Then I certainly called them openly,” continues the account of Noah's methods, and the verse shows the prophet varying his call to reach every heart. “Then I certainly called them openly,” or “thumma innee da'awtuhum jihara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah, after calling them privately, called them openly and publicly, and the believer is taught that the messenger employs every means of the call, the private and the public, and that the truth is to be proclaimed in the open, and that the public call is the call that reaches the many and leaves the many without excuse. Al-Tabari notes the word “jihar” — “openly,” “publicly” — and the believer is taught that the truth is not to be hidden, and that the message of God is a message for the open, and that the one who calls to the truth calls in the light and not in the corner.\n\nAl-Razi observes the wisdom of the variation, for the call which is made in the open reaches those whom the private call missed, and the believer is taught that the caller to the truth varies his means and his times, and that the variation is the mercy of the caller to the called, and that the one who calls in one way only has called one kind of heart and left the others uncalled. The verse also teaches the believer the courage of the messenger, for the open call is the call that invites the opposition, and the believer is taught that the messenger who calls openly is the messenger who has placed his cause in the hand of God, and that the believer is taught to proclaim the truth and to leave the consequence to the Lord of the proclamation. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to call to the truth openly and to vary his call, and to know that the call which is made in every way is the call which has done its duty, and that the response is with God, who opens the hearts He wills.",
  "9": "“then I surely preached to them publicly and privately,” completes the account of Noah's methods, and the verse joins the public call to the private in the full picture of the messenger's effort. “Then I surely preached to them publicly and privately,” or “thumma innee a'lantu lahum wa asrartu lahum israra.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah preached to his people openly and in secret, and the believer is taught that the messenger employs both the open proclamation and the private counsel, and that the private counsel is the counsel that reaches the heart of the one who is ashamed to turn in the open, and that the two together are the complete call that leaves no heart unapproached. Al-Tabari notes the two words — “a'lantu” and “asrartu” — and the believer is taught that the truth is to be spoken in the open and whispered in the private, and that the wise caller knows which heart is reached by which.\n\nAl-Razi observes the completeness of Noah's method, for the prophet had called by night and by day, in the open and in the secret, and the believer is taught that the one who calls to the truth must exhaust the means, and that the exhaustion of the means is the proof of the sincerity, and that the messenger who has tried every way is the messenger whose account before his Lord is complete. The verse also teaches the believer the wisdom of the private counsel, for there are hearts that the public call hardens and the private call softens, and the believer is taught to seek the heart in its own place, and to know that the wisdom of the call is the wisdom that reaches the called in the way that opens him. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to call with wisdom and with variety, to proclaim and to counsel, and to know that the call which has used every means is the call which has done its duty, and that the response is the matter of the Lord.",
  "10": "“saying, ‘Seek your Lord’s forgiveness, ˹for˺ He is truly Most Forgiving.” records the content of Noah's call, and the verse gives the invitation that the prophet made in the open and in the secret. “Saying, 'Seek your Lord's forgiveness, for He is truly Most Forgiving,'” or “fa-qultu istaghfiroo rabbakum innahu kana ghaffara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah invited his people to seek the forgiveness of their Lord, and the believer is taught that the seeking of the forgiveness is the door of every good, and that the one who seeks the forgiveness of God is the one upon whom the gates of the mercy open, and that the forgiveness is the first gift and the greatest. Al-Tabari notes the word “ghaffar” — “Most Forgiving,” the intensive form — and the believer is taught that the forgiveness of God is not a small forgiveness but an abounding one, and that the Lord who is “ghaffar” is the Lord whose forgiveness covers the sins of the one who turns to Him.\n\nAl-Razi observes the wisdom of beginning with the forgiveness, for the prophet who would win his people began with the mercy and not with the threat, and the believer is taught that the call to the truth is the call to the mercy, and that the heart which is won by the forgiveness is the heart which is then taught the obedience, and that the order of the call is the order of the mercy before the command. The verse also teaches the believer the value of “istighfar” — the seeking of forgiveness — for the seeking of the forgiveness is the turning of the heart, and the believer is taught to make the seeking of the forgiveness the constant of his tongue and his heart, and to know that the one who seeks the forgiveness of the Most Forgiving is the one whom the Most Forgiving covers. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to begin with the forgiveness, to seek it for himself and to invite others to it, and to know that the forgiveness is the first door and the widest.",
  "11": "“He will shower you with abundant rain,” states the first of the worldly rewards of the forgiveness, and the verse shows the connection between the turning of the heart and the opening of the sky. “He will shower you with abundant rain,” or “yursili as-sama'a 'alaykum midrara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the seeking of the forgiveness brings the rain, and the believer is taught that the sins of a people withhold the blessings, and that the turning of the people to the forgiveness opens the sky, and that the rain which is the mercy of God is the reward of the turning, and that the connection between the heart and the sky is the connection that the heedless do not see. Al-Tabari notes the word “midrar” — “abundant,” “pouring” — and the believer is taught that the reward of the forgiveness is the abundance, and that the sky which is opened for the repentant is the sky of the abundance.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the connection, for the verse teaches the believer that the affairs of the world are tied to the state of the hearts, and that the drought of the land is the mirror of the drought of the heart, and that the rain of the sky is the answer to the rain of the repentance, and the believer is taught to seek the rain of the sky through the rain of the heart, and to know that the one who seeks the forgiveness opens both the sky and the heart. The verse also teaches the believer the mercy of God, for the reward of the forgiveness is not delayed to the Hereafter alone but begins in this world, and the believer is taught to taste the reward of the turning in the rain and the abundance, and to know that the Hereafter holds the greater reward. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to connect his sins and his blessings, and to know that the drought of the blessing is the fruit of the drought of the heart, and that the seeking of the forgiveness is the door of the abundance in this world and the next.",
  "12": "“supply you with wealth and children, and give you gardens as well as rivers.” continues the rewards of the forgiveness, and the verse names the wealth and the children and the gardens and the rivers that follow the turning. “Supply you with wealth and children, and give you gardens as well as rivers,” or “wa yumdidkum bi-amwalin wa baneena wa yaj'al lakum jannatin wa yaj'al lakum anhara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the forgiveness brings the increase of wealth and children and the gift of gardens and rivers, and the believer is taught that the blessings of the world are the gifts of the Lord to the one who turns to Him, and that the turning of the heart is followed by the abundance of the hand, and that the wealth and the children and the gardens are the provisions that the Lord grants to the grateful. Al-Tabari notes the enumeration of the blessings, and the believer is taught that the Lord is the giver of every good, and that the one who seeks the forgiveness is the one upon whom the goods are poured.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the enumeration, for the verse names the very things that men desire — the wealth, the children, the gardens, the rivers — and the believer is taught that the desire for these is not the sin, and that the sin is the seeking of them without the Lord, and that the one who seeks the Lord first is given the world as the addition, and that the world which is sought for itself flees while the world which is sought through the Lord comes. The verse also teaches the believer the order of the seeking, for the forgiveness is sought first and the wealth follows, and the believer is taught to put the forgiveness first and to let the world follow, and to know that the one who puts the world first has reversed the order and lost both. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to seek the Lord and to trust Him for the world, and to know that the wealth and the children and the gardens are the gifts of the Lord, and that the one who is given them in the way of the gratitude is the one whose gifts are a blessing and not a trial.",
  "13": "“What is the matter with you that you are not in awe of the Majesty of Allah,” turns from the promises to the question, and the verse asks the people of Noah the question that exposes their state. “What is the matter with you that you are not in awe of the Majesty of Allah,” or “ma lakum la tarjoona lillahi waqara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah asked his people why they did not hold God in awe, and the believer is taught that the lack of the awe is the root of the heedlessness, and that the one who does not hold God in awe has lost the measure by which all things are measured, and that the awe of God is the beginning of the wisdom and the end of the recklessness. Al-Tabari notes the word “waqar” — “awe,” “dignity,” “majesty” — and the believer is taught that the Majesty of God is the thing that the heedless do not weigh, and that the one who weighs it is the one who is in awe, and that the awe is the guard of the heart.\n\nAl-Razi observes the force of the question, for the question “what is the matter with you” is the question of the one who is astonished by the heedlessness, and the believer is taught that the heedlessness of the heedless is the astonishment of the prophets, and that the one who sees the signs of God and does not awe is the one whose wonder is itself a wonder. The verse also teaches the believer the source of the awe, for the awe of God is born of the knowledge of God, and the believer is taught to know God through His signs and His favors so that the awe may grow, and to know that the awe which is not rooted in the knowledge is the awe that does not last. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to cultivate the awe of God, and to ask himself the question of Noah, and to know that the one who holds God in awe is the one whom God honors, and that the lack of the awe is the first step of the fall.",
  "14": "“when He truly created you in stages ˹of development˺?” grounds the awe in the fact of the creation, and the verse reminds the people of Noah of the stages of their own making. “When He truly created you in stages of development,” or “wa qad khalaqakum atwara.” Ibn Kathir explains that God created the human being in stages — from the dust to the drop, from the drop to the clot, from the clot to the flesh, and so on — and the believer is taught that the stages of the creation are the signs of the Creator, and that the one who reflects on his own origin is the one who is filled with the awe, and that the stages of the making are the proof of the Maker. Al-Tabari notes the word “atwar” — “stages,” “phases” — and the believer is taught that the creation of the human is not a single act but a series of acts, and that the series is the sign of the wisdom and the power of the Lord.\n\nAl-Razi observes the argument of the verse, for the one who is created in stages is the one who has a Creator, and the Creator is the one who deserves the awe, and the believer is taught that the reflection on the creation is the path to the awe, and that the one who does not awe has not reflected, and that the reflection on the stages of the self is the nearest of the reflections and the surest of the proofs. The verse also teaches the believer the dignity of the human creation, for the stages are the stages of a deliberate making, and the believer is taught to honor himself as the work of the Lord and to honor the Lord as the Maker of the work, and to know that the awe of the Maker is the first duty of the made. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to reflect on his own creation, and to let the reflection produce the awe, and to know that the one who knows his origin knows his Lord, and that the knowledge is the awe that the verse asks for.",
  "15": "“Do you not see how Allah created seven heavens, one above the other,” turns from the creation of the human to the creation of the heavens, and the verse extends the argument from the self to the sky. “Do you not see how Allah created seven heavens, one above the other,” or “a-lam taraw kayfa khalaqa Allahu sab'a samawatin tibaqa.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah pointed his people to the seven heavens, created in layers one above the other, and the believer is taught that the heavens are the great sign of the Creator, and that the one who looks up and sees the layered heavens is the one who sees the work of the Lord, and that the reflection on the sky is the reflection that fills the heart with the awe. Al-Tabari notes the word “tibaq” — “layers,” “one above the other” — and the believer is taught that the order of the heavens is the sign of the wisdom, and that the one who made the layers is the one who deserves the awe of the layers' beholder.\n\nAl-Razi observes the movement of the argument, for Noah moved from the stages of the human to the layers of the heavens, and the believer is taught that the signs of God are in the self and in the sky, and that the one who reflects on both has found the Lord in both, and that the argument from the creation is the argument that every human being can follow, for the self and the sky are the two books that every eye can read. The verse also teaches the believer the greatness of the creation, for the seven heavens are the seven layers of the divine making, and the believer is taught to look up with the awe of the one who sees the work, and to know that the Lord of the heavens is the Lord who is to be held in awe. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the sky as the book of the Lord, and to let the reading produce the awe, and to know that the one who sees the heavens and does not awe has seen the sight and missed the sign.",
  "16": "“placing the moon within them as a ˹reflected˺ light, and the sun as a ˹radiant˺ lamp?” continues the signs of the heavens with the moon and the sun, and the verse names the two lights that the eye knows. “Placing the moon within them as a reflected light, and the sun as a radiant lamp,” or “wa ja'ala al-qamara feehinna nooran wa ja'ala ash-shamsa siraja.” Ibn Kathir explains that God placed the moon in the heavens as a light and the sun as a lamp, and the believer is taught that the moon and the sun are the two lamps of the world, and that the one who made them is the one who deserves the awe, and that the light of the moon and the lamp of the sun are the gifts that the Lord has given to His servants for the measuring of their time and the lighting of their world. Al-Tabari notes the distinction between the moon and the sun, for the moon is the reflected light and the sun is the lamp, and the believer is taught that the distinction is the sign of the wisdom, and that the two lights serve the two purposes of the Lord.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the two lights, for the sun and the moon are the two signs that every people has seen, and the believer is taught that the signs which are nearest are the signs which are most neglected, and that the one who passes the sun and the moon without the awe has passed the two greatest of the visible signs, and that the reflection on the two lights is the reflection that awakens the heart. The verse also teaches the believer the mercy of the two lights, for the moon is the light of the night and the sun is the lamp of the day, and the believer is taught to thank the Lord of the two lights for the light that guides his steps and the lamp that warms his world, and to know that the thanks is the awe that the verse asks for. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the sun and the moon as the signs of the Lord, and to let the two lights lead him to the awe of the One who lit them, and to know that the one who sees the light and knows the Lord is the one whose heart is lit.",
  "17": "“Allah ˹alone˺ caused you to grow from the earth like a plant.” returns from the heavens to the earth, and the verse reminds the people of Noah of their own growth from the ground. “Allah alone caused you to grow from the earth like a plant,” or “wallahu anbatakum min al-ardi nabata.” Ibn Kathir explains that God caused the human being to grow from the earth, for the origin of the human is from the dust of the earth, and the believer is taught that the human being is a plant of the Lord, grown from the ground by His power, and that the one who knows his origin in the dust is the one who is humbled before the Lord of the dust, and that the humility is the awe that the verse seeks. Al-Tabari notes the word “anbatakum” — “He caused you to grow” — and the believer is taught that the growth of the human is the work of the Lord, and that the one who grows the plant of the human is the one who deserves the awe of the grown.\n\nAl-Razi observes the argument of the origin, for the human being who is grown from the earth is the human being who has a Grower, and the Grower is the Lord who is to be held in awe, and the believer is taught that the reflection on the origin is the cure of the pride, and that the one who remembers the dust of his origin is the one who cannot be arrogant, and that the remembrance of the dust is the beginning of the awe. The verse also teaches the believer the dignity of the growth, for the human is grown as a plant is grown, and the believer is taught that the growth is the sign of the care, and that the Lord who grows the plant of the human is the Lord who cares for the grown, and that the care of the Lord is the ground of the trust that the believer places in Him. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to remember his origin and to let the remembrance humble him, and to know that the one who grows from the dust and returns to the dust is the one who must hold the Grower in awe.",
  "18": "“Then He will return you to it, and then simply bring you forth ˹again˺.” completes the reminder of the origin with the reminder of the return, and the verse states the two ends of the human journey. “Then He will return you to it, and then simply bring you forth again,” or “thumma yu'eedukum feeha wa yukhrijukum ikhraja.” Ibn Kathir explains that God will return the human being to the earth in death and then bring him forth again in the resurrection, and the believer is taught that the human being is of the earth and to the earth and from the earth again, and that the return to the earth is the end of the first journey and the beginning of the second, and that the bringing forth again is the certainty that the heedless denied. Al-Tabari notes the two words — “yu'eedukum” and “yukhrijukum” — and the believer is taught that the return and the bringing forth are the two acts of the one Lord, and that the Lord who grew the human from the earth and returns him to it is the Lord who will bring him forth from it again.\n\nAl-Razi observes the argument of the return, for the one who grew the human from the earth is able to bring him forth again, and the believer is taught that the resurrection is the corollary of the creation, and that the one who denies the resurrection has denied the power that the creation proves, and that the reflection on the two ends — the dust and the return to the dust — is the reflection that establishes the certainty of the third end, the bringing forth. The verse also teaches the believer the seriousness of the return, for the return to the earth is the return that awaits every human being, and the believer is taught to prepare for the bringing forth while he is still on the earth, and to know that the one who lives in the heedlessness of the return is the one whom the return will surprise. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to remember the two ends and the third, and to let the remembrance prepare him, and to know that the Lord who grew him and will return him is the Lord who will bring him forth and judge him.",
  "19": "“And Allah ˹alone˺ spread out the earth for you” turns to the earth itself, and the verse names the spread of the earth as one of the signs that Noah's people were called to see. “And Allah alone spread out the earth for you,” or “wallahu ja'ala lakum al-arda bisata.” Ibn Kathir explains that God spread the earth out for the human being, and the believer is taught that the earth which is spread beneath the feet is the gift of the Lord, and that the spreading is the sign of the care, and that the one who walks upon the spread earth is the one who walks upon the mercy of the Lord, and that the walking should be a walking of gratitude. Al-Tabari notes the word “bisat” — “spread,” “carpet” — and the believer is taught that the earth is the carpet of the Lord, spread for the journey of His servants, and that the one who walks the carpet is the one who should remember the One who spread it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the earth, for the earth which is spread and stable is the sign of the power and the mercy, and the believer is taught that the stability of the earth is the gift that the heedless take for granted, and that the one who reflects on the spreading of the earth is the one who sees the hand of the Lord in the ground beneath his feet, and that the seeing is the awe that the verse asks for. The verse also teaches the believer the order of the signs, for the surah has moved from the heavens to the earth, and the believer is taught that the signs of God fill the whole of the creation, and that the one who looks up and looks down finds the Lord in both, and that the fullness of the signs is the fullness of the proof. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to walk the earth with the remembrance of the One who spread it, and to know that the spread earth is the gift of the Lord, and that the gift is to be received with the gratitude that the awe produces.",
  "20": "“to walk along its spacious pathways.’’” completes the sign of the earth with the paths that cross it, and the verse closes the argument of the signs with the call to walk them. “To walk along its spacious pathways,” or “li-taslukoo minha subulan fijaja.” Ibn Kathir explains that God made the pathways of the earth for the human being to walk, and the believer is taught that the roads and the passes of the earth are the provisions of the Lord for the journey of His servants, and that the one who walks the paths is the one who is carried by the mercy of the Lord, and that the walking is the sign of the journey that ends at the Lord. Al-Tabari notes the word “fijaj” — “spacious,” “wide passes” — and the believer is taught that the paths of the earth are wide and open, and that the opening is the mercy of the Lord who made the journey possible, and that the one who walks the open path is the one who should remember the One who opened it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the conclusion of the argument, for the surah has laid out the signs — the heavens, the moon, the sun, the growth from the earth, the return to it, the spreading of the earth, and the pathways — and the believer is taught that the signs are the summons to the awe, and that the one who has followed the signs from the sky to the path has arrived at the Lord, and that the arrival is the awe that Noah was asking for. The verse also teaches the believer the purpose of the earth, for the earth and its paths are made for the walking, and the believer is taught to walk the paths of the earth as the one who is journeying to the Lord, and to know that the path of the earth is the symbol of the path of the return, and that the one who walks the one should prepare for the other. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the path as the sign, and to walk with the remembrance of the One who made the path, and to know that the walking is the preparation for the meeting, and that the meeting is with the Lord of the paths.",
  "21": "“˹Eventually,˺ Noah cried, ‘My Lord! They have certainly persisted in disobeying me, and followed ˹instead˺ those ˹elite˺ whose ˹abundant˺ wealth and children only increase them in loss,” returns to the complaint of Noah, and the verse records the verdict of the prophet on his people after the long call. “Eventually, Noah cried, 'My Lord! They have certainly persisted in disobeying me, and followed instead those elite whose abundant wealth and children only increase them in loss,'” or “qala Noohun rabbi innahum 'asawnee wattaba'oo man lam yazidhu maluhu wa waladuhu illa khasara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah, after centuries of calling, turned to his Lord and reported that his people had persisted in disobeying him and had followed their leaders, the elite whose wealth and children only increased them in loss, and the believer is taught that the people of Noah followed their chiefs rather than their prophet, and that the following of the elite was the following of the wealth and the children that the elite possessed, and that the wealth and the children of the heedless are the very things that increase them in loss. Al-Tabari notes the word “khasar” — “loss” — and the believer is taught that the wealth which is not spent in the obedience and the children who are not raised in the faith are the wealth and the children of the loss.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the leaders, for the verse teaches the believer that the people are led by their elite, and that the elite who lead to the wrong are the elite whose wealth and children are the instruments of the misguidance, and that the believer is taught to weigh the leaders by the truth and not by the wealth, and to know that the leader who is wealthy in the world and poor in the truth is the leader who leads his followers to the loss. The verse also teaches the believer the danger of the wealth and the children, for the wealth and the children are the blessings that may become the trials, and the believer is taught to make his wealth and his children the means of his nearness and not the means of his loss, and to know that the one whose wealth and children increase him in the obedience is the one whose wealth and children increase him in the gain. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to examine his following, and to follow the truth and not the wealth, and to know that the loss of the heedless is the loss of the one who followed the world instead of the Lord.",
  "22": "“and who have devised a tremendous plot,” continues the description of the leaders, and the verse names the plot that they devised against the truth. “And who have devised a tremendous plot,” or “wa makaroo makran kubbaran.” Ibn Kathir explains that the leaders of Noah's people devised a great plot against Noah and his followers, and the believer is taught that the leaders of the heedless are the plotters against the truth, and that the plot of the leaders is the work of the night, and that the plot is “kubbar” — tremendous — in the eyes of its makers, while it is nothing before the Lord of the truth. Al-Tabari notes the word “kubbar” — the intensive of “great” — and the believer is taught that the plot of the heedless is great in their own estimation, and that the greatness of the plot is the measure of their heedlessness, and that the one who plots against the truth plots against the One who guards the truth.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the plot, for the verse teaches the believer that the truth is always met with the plot, and that the plotters are the leaders who fear the truth for what it will do to their standing, and that the believer is taught to recognize the plot and not to fear it, for the plot of the heedless is the plot of the weak, and the truth which the plot opposes is the truth which the plot cannot undo. The verse also teaches the believer the contrast between the plot and the trust, for the leaders plotted and Noah trusted, and the believer is taught to meet the plot of the heedless with the trust in the Lord, and to know that the trust of the believer is the answer to the plot of the disbeliever, and that the answer is the stronger. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to leave the plotters to their plot, and to place his trust in the Lord of the truth, and to know that the plot which is devised in the night is undone by the light of the Lord.",
  "23": "“urging ˹their followers˺, ‘Do not abandon your idols— especially Wadd, Suwâ’, Yaghûth, Ya’ûq, and Nasr.’” records the content of the leaders' plot, and the verse names the idols that they urged their followers to keep. “Urging their followers, 'Do not abandon your idols — especially Wadd, Suwa', Yaghuth, Ya'uq, and Nasr,'” or “wa qaloo la tatharunna alihatakum wa la tatharunna Waddan wa la Suwa'an wa la Yaghutha wa Ya'ooqa wa Nasra.” Ibn Kathir explains that the leaders urged the people to hold fast to their idols, naming the five greatest of them — Wadd, Suwa', Yaghuth, Ya'uq, and Nasr — and the believer is taught that these five were the names of righteous men of old whose images were later worshipped, and that the idolatry of the people of Noah was the corruption of the veneration of the dead, and that the leaders made the idols the banner under which they gathered the people against the truth. Al-Tabari notes the names and their origins, and the believer is taught that the idols of the nations are the inventions of the heedless, and that the invention is passed down until it is worshipped, and that the one who worships the invention has worshipped the work of his own hands.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the names, for the Quran preserves the names of the idols as the warning of the ages, and the believer is taught that the idols of every age have their names and their advocates, and that the names change while the idolatry remains, and that the believer is taught to recognize the idols of his own age and to refuse them, whatever their names. The verse also teaches the believer the method of the leaders, for the leaders urged the people to “not abandon” their idols, and the believer is taught that the call to hold the old is the call of the heedless, and that the truth is the call to the new life, and that the believer is taught to abandon what must be abandoned and to hold what must be held. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear the names of the idols as the names of his own possible attachments, and to know that the attachment to the old which blocks the truth is the idolatry that the verse warns against.",
  "24": "“Those ˹elite˺ have already led many astray. So ˹O Lord˺, only allow the wrongdoers to stray farther away.’” records the conclusion of Noah's account of his people, and the verse turns the complaint into the prayer of the prophet. “Those elite have already led many astray. So O Lord, only allow the wrongdoers to stray farther away,” or “wa qad adalloo katheeran wa la tazidi ath-thalimeena illa dalala.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah reported that the leaders had led many astray, and then prayed that the wrongdoers be increased in straying, and the believer is taught that this prayer of Noah was the prayer of the prophet after the long patience, and that the prophet, having exhausted every means of the call, left the wrongdoers to their straying and asked God to complete their ruin, and that the prayer is the prayer of the one whose duty is done and whose patience is spent. Al-Tabari notes that the prayer was made after the revelation that none more of his people would believe, and the believer is taught that the prophet prays for the destruction of the wrongdoers only when the matter has been decided and the door of the turning has closed.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the prayer, for the verse teaches the believer the difference between the patience of the prophet and the prayer of the prophet, and that the two belong to the two times, and that the one who prays for the ruin of the wrongdoers before his duty is done has confused the times, and that the believer is taught to call while the calling is of use and to leave the judgment to the Lord when the calling is done. The verse also teaches the believer the justice of the increase, for the increase in the straying is the increase of the punishment, and the believer is taught that the straying which is chosen is the straying which is increased, and that the wrongdoers who chose the darkness are given the darkness in full. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to call with the hope while the door is open, and to leave the judgment to the Lord when the door is closed, and to know that the Lord who opens and closes is the Lord of the just increase.",
  "25": "“So because of their sins, they were drowned, then admitted into the Fire. And they found none to help them against Allah.” states the end of Noah's people, and the verse records the drowning and what followed it. “So because of their sins, they were drowned, then admitted into the Fire. And they found none to help them against Allah,” or “f-ughriqoo fa-udkhiloo naran fa-lam yajidoo lahum min dooni Allahi ansara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the people of Noah were drowned because of their sins, and then admitted into the Fire, and the believer is taught that the drowning was the punishment of this world and the Fire is the punishment of the next, and that the two punishments are the two installments of the one account, and that the sins which drowned them in the water burn them in the Fire. Al-Tabari notes the sequence of the verse, for the drowning came first and the Fire after, and the believer is taught that the punishment of this world is not the whole of the account, and that the Hereafter holds the punishment that the world only began.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the two punishments, for the verse teaches the believer that death is not the end of the wrongdoer, and that the one who is drowned in the water is not saved by the drowning, and that the Fire which follows the drowning is the proof that the account continues beyond the death, and that the believer is taught to fear the account which the world only opens. The verse also teaches the believer the helplessness of the wrongdoers, for they found none to help them against God, and the believer is taught that the helpers of this world are the helpers of this world only, and that the one who is against God is alone against the Lord of all, and that the loneliness of the wrongdoer is the last of his punishments. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the two punishments and to seek the help of the one Helper, and to know that the one who has God has the help that endures, while the one who is without God is without help in the water and in the Fire.",
  "26": "“Noah had prayed, ‘My Lord! Do not leave a single disbeliever on earth.” records the final prayer of Noah, and the verse opens the last movement of the surah, in which the prophet prays for the end of the disbelievers. “Noah had prayed, 'My Lord! Do not leave a single disbeliever on earth,'” or “wa qala Noohun rabbi la tathar 'ala al-ardi min al-kafireena dayyara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah, after the long patience and after the revelation that his people would not believe further, prayed that no disbeliever be left on the earth, and the believer is taught that the prayer of Noah was the prayer of the prophet whose people had exhausted every mercy, and that the prayer was answered with the flood that covered the earth and left none of the disbelievers. Al-Tabari notes the word “dayyar” — “a single dweller” — and the believer is taught that the prayer was for the total removal of the disbelievers, and that the totality was the answer to the totality of their rejection, and that the prophet who had called every way now prayed for the end of those who had refused every way.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the prayer, for the verse teaches the believer that there is a point at which the patience of the prophet gives way to the prayer of the judgment, and that the point is the point at which the door of the turning has closed by the decree of God, and that the believer is taught not to hasten to the prayer of the judgment before the decree, and to know that the prayer of Noah was the prayer of the one who had been told that his people would not believe further. The verse also teaches the believer the seriousness of the rejection, for the rejection of the call which has exhausted every means is the rejection which leaves no hope, and the believer is taught to answer the call while the hope remains, and to know that the one who refuses the call until the hope is gone has refused the last mercy. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to answer the call while the caller calls, and to know that the prayer of the prophet for the end is the prayer that follows the closing of the door, and that the believer is taught to enter the door before it closes.",
  "27": "“For if You spare ˹any of˺ them, they will certainly mislead Your servants, and give birth only to ˹wicked˺ sinners, staunch disbelievers.” states the reason for Noah's prayer, and the verse gives the prophet's judgment on what the disbelievers would leave behind. “For if You spare any of them, they will certainly mislead Your servants, and give birth only to wicked sinners, staunch disbelievers,” or “innaka in tatharhum yudilloo 'ibadaka wa la yalidoo illa fajiran kaffara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah prayed for the removal of the disbelievers because, if any were spared, they would mislead the servants of God and would beget only wicked disbelievers, and the believer is taught that Noah's judgment was the judgment of the prophet who had known his people for centuries, and that the corruption of the disbelievers had reached the point where their offspring would inherit their corruption, and that the prayer for the end was the prayer for the protection of the future. Al-Tabari notes the word “fajir” and “kaffar” — “wicked sinner” and “staunch disbeliever” — and the believer is taught that the corruption of the parents is inherited by the children, and that the prophet saw in the disbelievers the fathers of a future of corruption.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the verse for the believer's understanding of the generations, for the verse teaches that the character of the parents shapes the character of the children, and that the community which is corrupt begets the corrupt, and that the prophet who prayed for the end of the corrupt was praying for the protection of the generations to come, and that the believer is taught to build his own generation upon the faith, and to know that the faith which is planted in the children is the faith which protects the future. The verse also teaches the believer the mercy within the severity, for the prayer of Noah was not the prayer of the vengeful but the prayer of the protector, and the believer is taught to see in the severity of the prophet the mercy of the shepherd who removes the diseased flock that the healthy may live. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the inheritance of the corruption, and to plant the faith in his children, and to know that the generation which is raised in the faith is the generation which is saved from the fate of the generations of Noah.",
  "28": "“My Lord! Forgive me, my parents, and whoever enters my house in faith, and ˹all˺ believing men and women. And increase the wrongdoers only in destruction.’” closes the surah with the final prayer of Noah, and the verse turns from the prayer for the end of the disbelievers to the prayer for the forgiveness of the believers. “My Lord! Forgive me, my parents, and whoever enters my house in faith, and all believing men and women. And increase the wrongdoers only in destruction,” or “rabbi ighfir lee wa li-walidayya wa li-man dakhala baytiya mu'minan wa lil-mu'mineena wal-mu'minat wa la tazidi ath-thalimeena illa tabara.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah prayed for the forgiveness of himself and his parents and the believers who entered his house and all the believing men and women, and the believer is taught that the prayer of the prophet gathers the whole community of the faith, and that the prayer for the forgiveness is the prayer that covers the living and the dead, and that the prophet who prayed for the end of the disbelievers prayed for the forgiveness of the believers, and that the two prayers are the two faces of the one heart. Al-Tabari notes the inclusion of the parents, and the believer is taught that the prayer for the parents is the duty of the child, and that the believer is taught to pray for his parents and his household and the believers, and to make the prayer of Noah his own.\n\nAl-Razi observes the structure of the closing, for the surah which began with the warning ends with the prayer, and the believer is taught that the message of the prophet is the message of the mercy, and that the warning which opened the surah is completed by the prayer which closes it, and that the prophet who warned his people is the prophet who prayed for the believers, and that the two acts are the two halves of the mission. The verse also teaches the believer the prayer for the increase, for Noah prayed that the wrongdoers be increased only in destruction, and the believer is taught that the increase of the wrongdoers in the destruction is the answer to their increase in the wrong, and that the believer is taught to pray for the believers and to leave the wrongdoers to the just increase of the Lord. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to close his reading of the surah with the prayer of Noah, to pray for the forgiveness of himself and the believers, and to know that the prayer of the prophet is the prayer of the community, and that the one who prays it joins the ark of the forgiven."
};
